A: I do not see a PexObserve line in the code for the assertions, so those are likely not being generated. Perhaps you are looking for something similar to:
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("old_arrList_x", Old_x);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

This will make the values of the variables visible in the Visual Studio Visualizer, but does not ensure anything about them.
If you want the assertion to fail if these values does not meet the criteria, you would be looking for an Assert.IsTrue statement:
Assert.IsTrue(
    New_arrListContainsX 
    && New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 
    && New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0 
    && New_Ret == New_arrListCount - 1);

This will fail the test if New_arrListCount, New_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX, or New_Ret do not meet the specified criteria.
If the array list is empty and you're trying to pop it, your test will fail. Perhaps you should add a special case for the empty array list.
Remember, your test logic must still pass (according to actual execution), for Pex to generate a new test case.
Q: How to add a new row to an Access table through SSIS package? I have an SSIS package that reads data from an Excel file (using Excel Source) and puts it onto a temporary SQL Server table (using OLE DB Destination).
I have a requirement to add a new row to an Access table.
It seems impossible, considering the following error messages:

SSIS Error Code DTS_E_CANNOTACQUIRECONNECTIONFROMCONNECTIONMANAGER. The
  AcquireConnection method call failed for the connection manager
  "myConnectionManager". This failure may have been caused by a Connectio
  nManager property that is not valid or that is set to a invalid
  connection string or a connection manager that is not configured for
  connection reuse.

I'm only working with an SQL Server database, I cannot switch to an Access database but I can edit the properties of my connection, my questions are:
1. Can SSIS add a new row to an Access database?
2. If yes, how is it accomplished?
Thanks.

A: The error you're seeing might stem from some misconfiguration, but it's less clear why SQL Server can connect but not Access. 
You can perform operations with Excel source and OLE DB destination in SSIS, including inserting or updating. 
Here is a quick guide which explains how you can do this:


*

*Insert: In your OLE DB destination, you can use the "Inserting a Record in a Table" option

*Update: Create another data flow in your SSIS package for updating records. Create an Excel source, an OLE DB Destination to connect to your Access database (Jump to the "Importing results" in this guide for more details), and finally an OLE DB Destination connected to a "Table" (not a view). Make sure to set the "TableName" property of the OLE DB Destination to the name of the table you want to update.


In summary, you can manage Access database insertion and update using SSIS in this way. However, note that SSIS package needs "Microsoft Access Driver (*.mdb)" or "Microsoft Excel driver (*.xls)" and not "Microsoft Office 12.0 Access Driver (*.mdb, *.accdb)" or Excel driver to connect to Access, or you get the error you described.
Moreover, SSIS needs the right paths and ACE drivers setup with the right permissions. With Excel drivers setup you will not get the error for Access connections but you will still get error on Access's path and drivers. 
It's still worth noting that--------------------            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListIndexOfX);
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            Assert.AreEqual(true, New_arrListContainsX);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ new array does not contain the default value for type T
            Assert.AreNotEqual(-1, New_Ret);
	    Assert.AreEqual(x,arrList[New_Ret]);
        }
    }
}

